11
00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:44.282
Introduction and Why It Matters.

12
00:00:45.532 --> 00:00:48.561
A story: flush the cache so the sale prices show up.

13
00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:53.961
At 7:59 pm on sale day, the pricing team loaded the festival prices.

14
00:00:54.241 --> 00:01:04.225
To make them show up immediately, someone ran FLUSHALL on the catalog Redis cluster at 8:00:00, the exact second the sale opened.

15
00:01:04.505 --> 00:01:05.401
Stampede.

16
00:01:05.681 --> 00:01:12.700
110 K req per second that had been served ~98 % from cache now all missed at once.

17
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.137
Every miss ran the same 8 ms product-card query.

18
00:01:16.417 --> 00:01:24.439
The catalog PostgreSQL went from 2 K to 60 K queries per second and hit 100 % CPU in seconds.

19
00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:30.713
Queries slowed to 2 s, and the cache couldn't refill because every loader was stuck waiting on the database.

20
00:01:32.063 --> 00:01:33.557
Stale prices anyway.

21
00:01:33.837 --> 00:01:40.535
Each catalog pod also had an in-process cache (Caffeine, 10-minute TTL) that FLUSHALL didn't touch.

22
00:01:40.815 --> 00:01:48.282
For ten minutes, customers saw old prices on some requests and new prices on others, depending on which pod served them.

23
00:01:48.562 --> 00:01:52.487
The cart re-priced at checkout, and customers saw price changed errors.

24
00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.282
One stale price forever.

25
00:01:54.562 --> 00:02:04.247
A price update raced with a cache fill: a reader loaded the old price, the writer updated the DB and deleted the key, and then the reader wrote the old price back.

26
00:02:04.527 --> 00:02:08.133
It stayed until the Redis TTL expired six hours later.

27
00:02:09.483 --> 00:02:21.045
Every one of these is a caching design failure, not a database failure: no stampede protection, no invalidation across cache layers, and no protection against the read or write race.

28
00:02:22.395 --> 00:02:23.078
Quote:

29
00:02:23.358 --> 00:02:25.022
The thesis of this chapter.

30
00:02:25.302 --> 00:02:29.441
A cache is a copy of data with a lifetime and a staleness contract.

31
00:02:29.721 --> 00:02:32.174
Caching is easy to add and hard to get right.

32
00:02:32.454 --> 00:02:39.771
The hard parts are what happens on a miss storm, how copies are invalidated, and what staleness each reader is allowed to see.

