00:08:23.211 --> 00:08:24.341
Caching patterns.

165
00:08:24.621 --> 00:08:25.987
Here is a quick table:

166
00:08:26.267 --> 00:08:30.896
Pattern, and Read path, and Write path, and Pros, and Cons, and Grocery use.

167
00:08:31.176 --> 00:08:33.011
Cache-aside (lazy loading).

168
00:08:33.291 --> 00:08:34.229
Read path:

169
00:08:34.509 --> 00:08:38.989
App checks the cache, and on a miss loads from the DB and populates the cache.

170
00:08:39.269 --> 00:08:40.187
Write path:

171
00:08:40.467 --> 00:08:43.155
App writes the DB, then deletes the cache key.

172
00:08:43.435 --> 00:08:44.288
Pros:

173
00:08:44.568 --> 00:08:48.728
Simple; cache only holds what's read; cache failure doesn't block reads.

174
00:08:49.008 --> 00:08:49.819
Cons:

175
00:08:50.099 --> 00:08:54.664
First read is slow; race conditions (§2.8); stampedes.

176
00:08:54.944 --> 00:08:55.947
Grocery use:

177
00:08:56.227 --> 00:08:58.104
Product cards, store prices.

178
00:08:58.384 --> 00:08:59.344
Read-through.

179
00:08:59.624 --> 00:09:00.563
Read path:

180
00:09:00.843 --> 00:09:03.488
Cache library loads on a miss (loader function).

181
00:09:03.768 --> 00:09:04.685
Write path:

182
00:09:04.965 --> 00:09:06.139
As cache-aside.

183
00:09:06.419 --> 00:09:07.272
Pros:

184
00:09:07.552 --> 00:09:09.771
Cleaner code; enables single-flight.

185
00:09:10.051 --> 00:09:10.861
Cons:

186
00:09:11.141 --> 00:09:12.507
Same staleness issues.

187
00:09:12.787 --> 00:09:13.789
Grocery use:

188
00:09:14.069 --> 00:09:15.712
Caffeine LoadingCache L1.

189
00:09:17.062 --> 00:09:17.979
Write-through.

190
00:09:18.259 --> 00:09:19.198
Read path:

191
00:09:19.478 --> 00:09:21.249
Cache is always populated.

192
00:09:21.529 --> 00:09:22.446
Write path:

193
00:09:22.726 --> 00:09:25.009
Write to cache and DB synchronously.

194
00:09:25.289 --> 00:09:26.142
Pros:

195
00:09:26.422 --> 00:09:28.214
Reads always hit after writes.

196
00:09:28.494 --> 00:09:29.305
Cons:

197
00:09:29.585 --> 00:09:34.961
Write latency; caches data nobody reads; still inconsistent if one write fails.

198
00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:36.243
Grocery use:

199
00:09:36.523 --> 00:09:38.230
Rarely: config values.

200
00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.089
Write-behind (write-back).

201
00:09:40.369 --> 00:09:41.307
Read path:

202
00:09:41.587 --> 00:09:42.825
Reads from the cache.

203
00:09:43.105 --> 00:09:44.022
Write path:

204
00:09:44.302 --> 00:09:47.310
Write to the cache; flush to the DB asynchronously.

205
00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:48.443
Pros:

206
00:09:48.723 --> 00:09:50.430
Very fast writes; batching.

207
00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:51.521
Cons:

208
00:09:51.801 --> 00:09:55.875
Data loss on cache failure; ordering and consistency complexity.

209
00:09:56.155 --> 00:09:57.158
Grocery use:

210
00:09:57.438 --> 00:10:00.254
Counters for analytics (view counts), never money.

211
00:10:00.534 --> 00:10:01.579
Refresh-ahead.

212
00:10:01.859 --> 00:10:02.798
Read path:

213
00:10:03.078 --> 00:10:05.809
Serve from the cache; refresh before expiry.

214
00:10:06.089 --> 00:10:06.942
Pros:

215
00:10:07.222 --> 00:10:08.545
Hot keys never miss.

216
00:10:08.825 --> 00:10:09.635
Cons:

217
00:10:09.915 --> 00:10:12.347
Refreshes data that may not be read again.

218
00:10:12.627 --> 00:10:13.630
Grocery use:

219
00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:17.366
Hot SKUs during sales; GST rate tables.

220
00:10:18.716 --> 00:10:19.356
Tip:

221
00:10:19.636 --> 00:10:22.025
Delete, don't update, on writes (cache-aside).

222
00:10:22.305 --> 00:10:26.444
After a DB write, delete the cache key rather than writing the new value.

223
00:10:26.724 --> 00:10:33.508
Two concurrent writers updating the cache can leave the older value last (their DB order and cache order differ).

224
00:10:33.788 --> 00:10:36.839
Deleting means the next reader loads the current value.

225
00:10:37.119 --> 00:10:41.961
That's not the whole story, though: see the read or write race in §2.8.

226
00:10:43.311 --> 00:10:44.613
Eviction policies.

227
00:10:44.893 --> 00:10:46.258
Here is a quick table:

228
00:10:46.538 --> 00:10:49.119
Policy, and Evicts, and Good for, and Weakness.

229
00:10:49.399 --> 00:10:50.317
LRU.

230
00:10:50.597 --> 00:10:51.386
Evicts:

231
00:10:51.666 --> 00:10:53.074
Least recently used.

232
00:10:53.354 --> 00:10:54.186
Good for:

233
00:10:54.466 --> 00:10:55.981
Recency-heavy workloads.

234
00:10:56.261 --> 00:10:57.114
Weakness:

235
00:10:57.394 --> 00:11:00.530
A one-off scan (bulk export) flushes hot items.

236
00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:01.685
LFU.

237
00:11:01.965 --> 00:11:02.754
Evicts:

238
00:11:03.034 --> 00:11:04.421
Least frequently used.

239
00:11:04.701 --> 00:11:05.533
Good for:

240
00:11:05.813 --> 00:11:08.266
Stable popularity (top SKUs).

241
00:11:08.546 --> 00:11:09.399
Weakness:

242
00:11:09.679 --> 00:11:13.221
Slow to adapt; old popular items linger (use aging).

243
00:11:13.501 --> 00:11:15.421
W-TinyLFU (Caffeine).

244
00:11:15.701 --> 00:11:16.490
Evicts:

245
00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.842
Admission by frequency sketch + windowed LRU.

246
00:11:20.122 --> 00:11:20.954
Good for:

247
00:11:21.234 --> 00:11:23.645
Mixed workloads; near-optimal hit rates.

248
00:11:23.925 --> 00:11:24.778
Weakness:

249
00:11:25.058 --> 00:11:27.810
(the best default for JVM L1 caches).

250
00:11:28.090 --> 00:11:29.733
TTL or TTI.

251
00:11:30.013 --> 00:11:30.802
Evicts:

252
00:11:31.082 --> 00:11:33.941
Expired entries (time-to-live or time-to-idle).

253
00:11:34.221 --> 00:11:35.053
Good for:

254
00:11:35.333 --> 00:11:36.527
Bounding staleness.

255
00:11:36.807 --> 00:11:37.661
Weakness:

256
00:11:37.941 --> 00:11:39.946
Not a memory policy by itself.

257
00:11:40.226 --> 00:11:41.015
Random.

258
00:11:41.295 --> 00:11:42.085
Evicts:

259
00:11:42.365 --> 00:11:43.474
Random entry.

260
00:11:43.754 --> 00:11:44.586
Good for:

261
00:11:44.866 --> 00:11:45.783
Very cheap.

262
00:11:46.063 --> 00:11:46.917
Weakness:

263
00:11:47.197 --> 00:11:48.242
Unpredictable.

264
00:11:49.592 --> 00:12:03.715
Redis maxmemory-policy: allkeys-lru or allkeys-lfu for pure caches, volatile- when only keys with TTLs may be evicted, and noeviction for stateful data (writes fail instead of silently losing data).

265
00:12:05.065 --> 00:12:07.305
TTLs and the staleness contract.

266
00:12:07.585 --> 00:12:13.430
Every cached item has a staleness contract: the maximum time a reader may see an old value.

