351
00:15:40.461 --> 00:15:41.186
Scope:

352
00:15:41.466 --> 00:15:42.319
Everywhere.

353
00:15:42.599 --> 00:15:43.367
Warming.

354
00:15:43.647 --> 00:15:44.309
How:

355
00:15:44.589 --> 00:15:46.445
Pre-populate before known peaks.

356
00:15:46.725 --> 00:15:47.450
Scope:

357
00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:50.333
Sales, deploys, cache node replacement.

358
00:15:50.613 --> 00:15:53.237
Load shedding or circuit breaker at origin.

359
00:15:53.517 --> 00:15:54.178
How:

360
00:15:54.458 --> 00:15:56.869
Protect the DB if misses spike anyway.

361
00:15:57.149 --> 00:15:57.874
Scope:

362
00:15:58.154 --> 00:15:59.946
Last line (Ch 18).

363
00:16:01.296 --> 00:16:02.896
XFetch (Vattani et al.

364
00:16:03.176 --> 00:16:06.483
VLDB 2015) refreshes early when:

365
00:16:07.833 --> 00:16:12.889
The formula: now - delta, beta, ln(rand()) ≥ expiry.

366
00:16:14.239 --> 00:16:19.679
where δ is the recompute time and β ≥ 1 is aggressiveness.

367
00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:28.172
From L1's run, with 10,000 req per second on one key, a 300 s TTL and δ = 0.2 s:

368
00:16:29.522 --> 00:16:33.106
On screen: at t=298.0s:

369
00:16:33.386 --> 00:16:39.423
1 of 10,000 callers would refresh early. at t=299.0s:

370
00:16:39.703 --> 00:16:46.082
64 of 10,000 callers would refresh early. at t=299.8s:

371
00:16:46.362 --> 00:16:51.375
3,667 of 10,000 callers would refresh early.

372
00:16:54.225 --> 00:17:02.972
In practice the first early refresh (~2 s before expiry) repopulates the key, so the key never actually expires under load.

373
00:17:03.252 --> 00:17:06.943
Combined with single-flight, only one caller per pod does the work.

374
00:17:08.293 --> 00:17:10.533
The cache-aside race and versioned writes.

