00:20:57.137 --> 00:20:58.631
Spring Cache abstraction.

451
00:20:58.911 --> 00:21:00.276
Here is a quick table:

452
00:21:00.556 --> 00:21:03.265
Annotation, and Meaning. @EnableCaching:

453
00:21:03.545 --> 00:21:10.180
Turns on cache proxies. @Cacheable(cacheNames = productCard, key = sku, sync = true):

454
00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:25.500
Cache-aside read. sync = true makes concurrent misses in the same JVM wait for one loader (single-flight per JVM, if the cache provider supports it). @CacheEvict(cacheNames = productCard, key = sku):

455
00:21:25.780 --> 00:21:27.657
Delete on write. @CachePut:

456
00:21:27.937 --> 00:21:31.713
Always execute and put the result (write-through style). @Caching:

457
00:21:31.993 --> 00:21:33.081
Combine several.

458
00:21:34.431 --> 00:21:35.370
Pitfalls:

459
00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:46.423
Proxies don't intercept self-invocation. @CacheEvict runs before the transaction commits, unless you defer it to after commit, which opens a stale-read window.

460
00:21:46.703 --> 00:21:50.351
There's no versioning, so the §2.8 race remains.

461
00:21:50.631 --> 00:21:53.127
Default Java serialization is fragile.

462
00:21:53.407 --> 00:21:55.498
Use JSON with explicit types.

463
00:21:55.778 --> 00:22:00.621
Null values get cached (or don't) depending on disableCachingNullValues.

464
00:22:01.971 --> 00:22:08.520
Keys need explicit namespaces and versions (v2::productCard::SKU) for schema changes.

