847
00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:38.249
An end-to-end test (Testcontainers Kafka + Redis + two app contexts as pods) publishes pricing.price.changed.v1 with version 9.

848
00:46:38.529 --> 00:46:49.601
It asserts that L2 holds a v9 tombstone within 1 s, both pods' L1 no longer return v8, and the next read on each pod returns the new price from the DB.

849
00:46:50.951 --> 00:46:51.698
Note:

850
00:46:51.978 --> 00:46:54.559
ADR-029 or 030:

851
00:46:54.839 --> 00:46:58.829
Versioned cache writes; event-driven invalidation; no flushes.

852
00:46:59.109 --> 00:47:00.026
Context.

853
00:47:00.306 --> 00:47:11.954
A cache flush at sale start caused a DB meltdown, L1 caches served stale prices across pods, and a read or write race pinned a stale price for hours (§1.1).

854
00:47:13.304 --> 00:47:14.179
Decision.

855
00:47:14.459 --> 00:47:19.621
(1) Every L2 value carries the SoR version, and writes use set-if-newer (Lua).

856
00:47:19.901 --> 00:47:22.504
Invalidations write version tombstones.

857
00:47:22.784 --> 00:47:26.112
(2) The SoR publishes change events via outbox.

858
00:47:26.392 --> 00:47:36.547
A fleet-wide invalidator writes the tombstones (including hot-key replicas) and purges the CDN by surrogate key, and per-pod consumers evict L1.

859
00:47:36.827 --> 00:47:40.901
(3) L1 TTL is at most 5 s for mutable data.

860
00:47:41.181 --> 00:47:48.989
L2 soft TTLs have ± 10 % jitter, with XFetch early refresh and serve-stale on refresh failure.

861
00:47:49.269 --> 00:47:56.267
(4) DB loads per pod are capped by a semaphore, and excess is shed with 503 + Retry-After.

862
00:47:56.547 --> 00:47:59.533
(5) Known events are pre-warmed from the event plan.

863
00:47:59.813 --> 00:48:03.995
FLUSHALL or FLUSHDB are prohibited in production (Redis ACL).

864
00:48:04.275 --> 00:48:06.365
(6) Caches serve displays only.

865
00:48:06.645 --> 00:48:09.141
Checkout, stock and payment read the SoR.

866
00:48:10.491 --> 00:48:11.515
Consequences.

867
00:48:11.795 --> 00:48:17.598
(+) Staleness at most ~1 to 5 s after a change, with no stale-forever values.

868
00:48:17.878 --> 00:48:21.206
(+) Cold or lost Redis doesn't take down the DB.

869
00:48:21.486 --> 00:48:26.371
(minus ) Lua scripts, invalidation events and per-pod consumers to operate.

870
00:48:26.651 --> 00:48:37.553
(minus ) Invalidation depends on outbox events: bulk SQL that bypasses the outbox must publish events too, and that's enforced by pricing owning all writes.

871
00:48:37.833 --> 00:48:49.097
Revisit when: invalidation lag or volume grows (consider CDC-driven cache population), or client-side caching with Redis tracking becomes attractive for L1.

