Slow loads enlarge stampede windows.

1013
00:53:54.743 --> 00:53:58.860
Single-flight waiters or permits exhausted (cache.load.rejected):

1014
00:53:59.140 --> 00:54:03.684
Stampede pressure; shedding in effect. cache.l2.setrejectedstale:

1015
00:54:03.964 --> 00:54:05.564
Races caught by versioning.

1016
00:54:05.844 --> 00:54:09.620
A non-zero count is expected; a spike means slow readers.

1017
00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:14.764
Invalidation lag (change committed, then tombstone written, then L1 evicted):

1018
00:54:15.044 --> 00:54:16.303
Freshness SLO.

1019
00:54:17.653 --> 00:54:19.807
Evictions, memory, keyspace size:

1020
00:54:20.087 --> 00:54:21.047
Capacity.

1021
00:54:21.327 --> 00:54:23.503
Per-shard ops per second and latency:

1022
00:54:23.783 --> 00:54:24.722
Hot keys.

1023
00:54:25.002 --> 00:54:29.226
CDN hit ratio, purge count and latency, origin fetches:

1024
00:54:29.506 --> 00:54:30.658
Edge effectiveness.

1025
00:54:30.938 --> 00:54:34.799
Sampled consistency check (cached version versus SoR version):

1026
00:54:35.079 --> 00:54:36.637
Detects silent staleness.

1027
00:54:37.987 --> 00:54:38.925
Security.

1028
00:54:39.205 --> 00:54:48.827
Never cache personalized responses at shared layers (CDN or gateway) unless the cache key includes the user or the response is truly public.

1029
00:54:49.107 --> 00:54:54.056
Cache-Control: private or no-store is the default for authenticated responses.

1030
00:54:54.336 --> 00:54:59.499
A single misconfigured public header can serve one user's cart or address to others.

1031
00:54:59.779 --> 00:55:09.507
Cache keys must include every input that changes the response (store, language, app version, experiment bucket) or users see each other's variants.

1032
00:55:10.857 --> 00:55:16.617
Web cache poisoning: unkeyed headers (X-Forwarded-Host) reflected into cached responses.

1033
00:55:16.897 --> 00:55:19.009
Normalize and validate at the edge.

1034
00:55:19.289 --> 00:55:27.353
Sensitive data in Redis: avoid PII where possible, use TLS and ACLs, and set TTLs on everything.

1035
00:55:27.633 --> 00:55:31.217
Remember that Redis snapshots and replicas copy data too.

1036
00:55:31.497 --> 00:55:38.750
Prohibit dangerous commands (FLUSHALL, FLUSHDB, KEYS, CONFIG) for application users via Redis ACLs.

1037
00:55:40.100 --> 00:55:41.764
Performance and tuning knobs.

1038
00:55:42.044 --> 00:55:43.409
Here is a quick table:

1039
00:55:43.689 --> 00:55:45.929
Knob, and Starting point, and Guidance.

1040
00:55:46.209 --> 00:55:48.556
L1 TTL (mutable data).

1041
00:55:48.836 --> 00:55:49.903
Starting point:

1042
00:55:50.183 --> 00:55:51.377
1 to 5 s.

1043
00:55:51.657 --> 00:55:52.553
Guidance:

1044
00:55:52.833 --> 00:55:55.841
Bounds cross-pod staleness if broadcasts are missed.

1045
00:55:56.121 --> 00:55:57.252
L1 size.

1046
00:55:57.532 --> 00:55:58.599
Starting point:

1047
00:55:58.879 --> 00:56:01.609
Working set of hot keys (10 to 50 K).

1048
00:56:01.889 --> 00:56:02.785
Guidance:

1049
00:56:03.065 --> 00:56:06.479
Watch heap and GC; Caffeine W-TinyLFU.

1050
00:56:06.759 --> 00:56:08.231
L2 soft TTL.

1051
00:56:08.511 --> 00:56:09.577
Starting point:

1052
00:56:09.857 --> 00:56:12.951
Minutes to hours, ± 10 % jitter.

1053
00:56:13.231 --> 00:56:14.127
Guidance:

1054
00:56:14.407 --> 00:56:16.668
Safety net; events do the freshness.

1055
00:56:16.948 --> 00:56:18.399
L2 hard TTL.

1056
00:56:18.679 --> 00:56:19.745
Starting point:

1057
00:56:20.025 --> 00:56:21.369
Soft + margin.

1058
00:56:21.649 --> 00:56:22.545
Guidance:

1059
00:56:22.825 --> 00:56:25.172
Allows serve-stale during refresh failures.

1060
00:56:25.452 --> 00:56:26.647
XFetch β.

1061
00:56:26.927 --> 00:56:27.993
Starting point:

1062
00:56:28.273 --> 00:56:29.383
1.0.

1063
00:56:29.663 --> 00:56:30.559
Guidance:

1064
00:56:30.839 --> 00:56:32.652
Higher means earlier refreshes.

1065
00:56:32.932 --> 00:56:34.489
DB permits per pod.

1066
00:56:34.769 --> 00:56:35.836
Starting point:

1067
00:56:36.116 --> 00:56:38.313
DB budget ÷ pods.

1068
00:56:38.593 --> 00:56:39.489
Guidance:

1069
00:56:39.769 --> 00:56:41.732
Little's Law (Ch 02).

1070
00:56:43.082 --> 00:56:44.682
Negative-cache TTL.

1071
00:56:44.962 --> 00:56:46.029
Starting point:

1072
00:56:46.309 --> 00:56:47.631
30 to 60 s.

1073
00:56:47.911 --> 00:56:48.807
Guidance:

1074
00:56:49.087 --> 00:56:52.330
Long enough to absorb abuse, short enough for new products.

1075
00:56:52.610 --> 00:56:53.890
Hot-key replicas.

1076
00:56:54.170 --> 00:56:55.237
Starting point:

1077
00:56:55.517 --> 00:56:56.711
4 to 16.

1078
00:56:56.991 --> 00:56:57.887
Guidance:

1079
00:56:58.167 --> 00:57:00.002
Number of shards to spread over.

1080
00:57:00.282 --> 00:57:01.562
Tombstone TTL.

1081
00:57:01.842 --> 00:57:07.197
Starting point: ≥ the longest plausible slow-reader delay (60 s).

1082
00:57:07.477 --> 00:57:08.373
Guidance:

1083
00:57:08.653 --> 00:57:10.359
Too short brings back the race.

1084
00:57:10.639 --> 00:57:11.578
CDN.

1085
00:57:11.858 --> 00:57:16.722
Starting point: s-maxage 30 to 300 s + stale-while-revalidate.

1086
00:57:17.002 --> 00:57:17.898
Guidance:

1087
00:57:18.178 --> 00:57:19.842
Surrogate keys for purges.

1088
00:57:21.192 --> 00:57:21.917
Cost.

1089
00:57:22.197 --> 00:57:26.037
A cache is memory traded for origin capacity and latency.

1090
00:57:26.317 --> 00:57:38.947
At 98 % hit ratio, the catalog DB serves ~4 K per second instead of 190 K per second, which is the difference between one modest cluster and a heavily sharded one.

1091
00:57:39.227 --> 00:57:42.363
Right-size by the working set, not the whole dataset.

1092
00:57:42.643 --> 00:57:51.219
A 500 MB card set plus 240 MB of prices in Redis costs far less than the DB capacity it replaces.

1093
00:57:51.499 --> 00:57:54.613
CDN egress is often the largest line item.

1094
00:57:54.893 --> 00:58:01.293
Cache images aggressively, with versioned immutable URLs and modern formats (WebP or AVIF).

1095
00:58:02.643 --> 00:58:07.891
Every cache layer adds operational cost: invalidation paths, metrics, runbooks.

1096
00:58:08.171 --> 00:58:12.310
Add a layer only when the estimate shows it's needed (Ch 02).

1097
00:58:13.660 --> 00:58:14.599
Part 7.

1098
00:58:14.879 --> 00:58:16.585
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1099
00:58:17.835 --> 00:58:18.475
1.

1100
00:58:18.755 --> 00:58:20.633
Flushing the cache to refresh data.

1101
00:58:20.913 --> 00:58:21.723
Symptom:

1102
00:58:22.003 --> 00:58:25.651
A stampede at the worst possible moment (§1.1).

1103
00:58:25.931 --> 00:58:26.657
Fix:

1104
00:58:26.937 --> 00:58:31.353
Event-driven invalidation of the changed keys, and warming before events.

1105
00:58:31.633 --> 00:58:33.147
Block FLUSH via ACLs.

1106
00:58:33.427 --> 00:58:34.131
2.

1107
00:58:34.411 --> 00:58:37.782
Updating the cache on write instead of deleting (or versioning).

1108
00:58:39.132 --> 00:58:49.116
Here is the Java code. before: two concurrent writers can leave the OLDER value in the cache. after: version tombstone; readers repopulate with set-if-newer.

1109
00:58:51.966 --> 00:58:52.713
3.

1110
00:58:52.993 --> 00:58:54.465
Evicting before commit.

1111
00:58:54.745 --> 00:58:55.555
Symptom:

1112
00:58:55.835 --> 00:58:59.718
A reader re-caches the old value between the evict and the commit.

1113
00:58:59.998 --> 00:59:00.723
Fix:

1114
00:59:01.003 --> 00:59:08.278
Invalidate after commit, via an outbox event or a TransactionSynchronization.afterCommit hook, plus versioning.

1115
00:59:08.558 --> 00:59:09.262
4.

1116
00:59:09.542 --> 00:59:11.078
No stampede protection.

1117
00:59:12.428 --> 00:59:22.241
Here is the Java code. before: every concurrent miss hits the DB. after: per-pod single-flight + early refresh + DB permits (L3).

1118
00:59:25.091 --> 00:59:25.859
5.

1119
00:59:26.139 --> 00:59:29.019
Same TTL for everything, with no jitter.

1120
00:59:29.299 --> 00:59:31.795
Synchronized expiry means an avalanche.

1121
00:59:32.075 --> 00:59:32.822
6.

1122
00:59:33.102 --> 00:59:35.619
Caching negative results forever, or never.

1123
00:59:35.899 --> 00:59:36.710
Symptom:

1124
00:59:36.990 --> 00:59:43.411
A product added later doesn't exist for hours, or bots probing random SKUs hammer the DB.

1125
00:59:43.691 --> 00:59:44.417
Fix:

1126
00:59:44.697 --> 00:59:49.731
Negative cache with a short TTL, plus an id validation or Bloom filter.

1127
00:59:50.011 --> 00:59:50.758
7.

1128
00:59:51.038 --> 00:59:53.086
Caching decisions, not displays.

1129
00:59:53.366 --> 00:59:54.177
Symptom:

1130
00:59:54.457 --> 00:59:59.833
Checkout charges a cached price, or allows a reservation based on a cached stock badge.

1131
01:00:00.113 --> 01:00:00.838
Fix:

1132
01:00:01.118 --> 01:00:05.513
Caches for display, the SoR for decisions (§2.12).

1133
01:00:05.793 --> 01:00:06.475
8.

1134
01:00:06.755 --> 01:00:09.401
Shared-edge caching of personalized responses.

1135
01:00:10.751 --> 01:00:17.193
Here is the HTTP code. before: public caching of a response that contains the user's address.

1136
01:00:20.043 --> 01:00:20.790
9.

1137
01:00:21.070 --> 01:00:23.267
Unversioned cache keys across deploys.

1138
01:00:23.547 --> 01:00:24.358
Symptom:

1139
01:00:24.638 --> 01:00:30.291
Deserialization errors or wrong fields after a payload change during a rolling deploy.

1140
01:00:30.571 --> 01:00:31.297
Fix:

1141
01:00:31.577 --> 01:00:36.761
A cache schema prefix (v1:, then v2:), with old keys expiring naturally.

1142
01:00:37.041 --> 01:00:37.745
10.

1143
01:00:38.025 --> 01:00:40.073
Just add a cache to hide a slow query.

1144
01:00:40.353 --> 01:00:41.163
Symptom:

1145
01:00:41.443 --> 01:00:47.374
The cache hides an N+1 or a missing index until the first cold start takes the DB down.

1146
01:00:47.654 --> 01:00:48.379
Fix:

1147
01:00:48.659 --> 01:00:52.222
Make the origin query fast (Ch 05) first.

1148
01:00:52.502 --> 01:00:55.510
The cache is for scale, not for fixing bad queries.

1149
01:00:56.860 --> 01:00:57.735
Part 8.

1150
01:00:58.015 --> 01:00:59.359
Interview Q and A.

1151
01:01:00.609 --> 01:01:01.441
Beginner.

1152
01:01:01.721 --> 01:01:02.745
Question 1.

1153
01:01:03.025 --> 01:01:05.777
What is caching, and how do you measure whether it's working?

1154
01:01:06.057 --> 01:01:07.422
Thirty-second answer:

1155
01:01:07.702 --> 01:01:14.571
Keeping copies of frequently read data in faster storage closer to the reader, to cut latency and origin load.

1156
01:01:14.851 --> 01:01:23.257
Measure it by hit ratio (request-weighted), origin load from misses, latency per layer, and freshness (how stale served data can be).

1157
01:01:24.607 --> 01:01:25.609
Question 2.

1158
01:01:25.889 --> 01:01:27.639
Where can you cache in a web system?

1159
01:01:27.919 --> 01:01:29.284
Thirty-second answer:

1160
01:01:29.564 --> 01:01:40.636
Client (browser or app), CDN edge, gateway or BFF, in-process in the service (L1), distributed cache (L2, Redis), and the database's own buffer cache.

1161
01:01:40.916 --> 01:01:43.967
Closer layers are faster but harder to invalidate.

1162
01:01:45.317 --> 01:01:46.362
Question 3.

1163
01:01:46.642 --> 01:01:49.095
LRU versus LFU: which and when?

1164
01:01:49.375 --> 01:01:50.741
Thirty-second answer:

1165
01:01:51.021 --> 01:01:58.445
LRU evicts the least recently used item, which suits recency-driven access but is vulnerable to one-off scans.

1166
01:01:58.725 --> 01:02:04.250
LFU evicts the least frequently used, which suits stable popularity but adapts slowly.

1167
01:02:04.530 --> 01:02:10.077
Caffeine's W-TinyLFU combines both and is the best default on the JVM.

1168
01:02:11.427 --> 01:02:12.493
Question 4.

1169
01:02:12.773 --> 01:02:14.416
How do you choose a TTL?

1170
01:02:14.696 --> 01:02:16.061
Thirty-second answer:

1171
01:02:16.341 --> 01:02:22.891
From the staleness contract, meaning how stale this data may be for its readers, and whether events invalidate it.

1172
01:02:23.171 --> 01:02:27.565
With event-driven invalidation, the TTL is a safety net (longer).

1173
01:02:27.845 --> 01:02:31.003
Without it, the TTL is the freshness bound (shorter).

1174
01:02:31.283 --> 01:02:32.499
Always add jitter.

1175
01:02:33.849 --> 01:02:34.851
Intermediate.

1176
01:02:35.131 --> 01:02:36.241
Question 5.

1177
01:02:36.521 --> 01:02:40.937
Cache-aside versus read-through versus write-through versus write-behind?

1178
01:02:41.217 --> 01:02:42.582
Thirty-second answer:

1179
01:02:42.862 --> 01:02:46.211
Cache-aside: the app loads on a miss and deletes on write.

1180
01:02:46.491 --> 01:02:47.899
It's simple and common.

1181
01:02:48.179 --> 01:02:52.297
Read-through: the cache library loads on a miss (enables single-flight).

1182
01:02:52.577 --> 01:02:55.051
Write-through: write the cache and DB together.

1183
01:02:55.331 --> 01:02:59.363
Reads are always warm, but writes are slower and cache unread data.

1184
01:02:59.643 --> 01:03:02.843
Write-behind: write the cache, flush to the DB later.

1185
01:03:03.123 --> 01:03:06.259
Very fast, but risks data loss, so never for money.

1186
01:03:07.609 --> 01:03:08.719
Question 6.

1187
01:03:08.999 --> 01:03:11.708
What is a cache stampede, and how do you prevent it?

1188
01:03:11.988 --> 01:03:13.353
Thirty-second answer:

1189
01:03:13.633 --> 01:03:20.033
Many concurrent misses on the same key (expiry, flush, cold start) all hit the origin at once.

1190
01:03:20.313 --> 01:03:33.924
Prevent it with single-flight (coalescing), probabilistic early refresh (XFetch), stale-while-revalidate, TTL jitter, warming, distributed locks for the hottest keys, and origin protection (permits and shedding).

1191
01:03:35.274 --> 01:03:36.362
Question 7.

1192
01:03:36.642 --> 01:03:40.162
How do you keep a cache consistent with the database on updates?

1193
01:03:40.442 --> 01:03:41.807
Thirty-second answer:

1194
01:03:42.087 --> 01:03:51.389
Write the DB (the SoR), then invalidate the cache after commit, ideally from a change event (outbox or CDC) so no update is missed.

1195
01:03:51.669 --> 01:03:53.503
Readers repopulate on a miss.

1196
01:03:53.783 --> 01:03:58.839
Protect against the reader or writer race with versioned conditional sets or leases.

1197
01:03:59.119 --> 01:04:03.087
Bound the worst case with TTLs, and don't use caches for decisions.

1198
01:04:04.437 --> 01:04:05.461
Question 8.

1199
01:04:05.741 --> 01:04:07.896
What are the pitfalls of Spring's @Cacheable?

1200
01:04:08.176 --> 01:04:09.541
Thirty-second answer:

1201
01:04:09.821 --> 01:04:15.965
Self-invocation bypasses the proxy. @CacheEvict may run before the transaction commits.

1202
01:04:16.245 --> 01:04:22.368
There's no versioning, so the race remains. sync = true only coalesces within one JVM.

1203
01:04:22.648 --> 01:04:24.675
Default serialization is fragile.

1204
01:04:24.955 --> 01:04:27.003
Null handling needs to be deliberate.

1205
01:04:27.283 --> 01:04:31.976
Keys need namespacing and versioning, and there's no multi-level coordination.

1206
01:04:33.326 --> 01:04:34.393
Question 9.

1207
01:04:34.673 --> 01:04:38.705
How do HTTP caching and CDNs work for APIs?

1208
01:04:38.985 --> 01:04:40.350
Thirty-second answer:

1209
01:04:40.630 --> 01:04:58.337
Cache-Control (max-age for browsers, s-maxage for shared caches, stale-while-revalidate, private or no-store), validators (ETag or If-None-Match, then 304), Vary for request-dependent variants, and CDN purges by URL or surrogate key.

1210
01:04:59.687 --> 01:05:00.455
Senior.

1211
01:05:00.735 --> 01:05:01.780
Question 10.

1212
01:05:02.060 --> 01:05:05.495
Explain the cache-aside race condition and how you'd fix it.

1213
01:05:05.775 --> 01:05:07.140
Thirty-second answer:

1214
01:05:07.420 --> 01:05:10.727
A reader misses and loads the old value from the DB.

1215
01:05:11.007 --> 01:05:13.993
A writer updates the DB and deletes the cache key.

1216
01:05:14.273 --> 01:05:18.945
The reader then writes the old value into the cache, where it stays until the TTL.

1217
01:05:19.225 --> 01:05:31.065
Fix it with versioned conditional writes: store the SoR version and accept a set only if its version ≥ the stored one, with invalidation writing a tombstone at the new version.

1218
01:05:31.345 --> 01:05:37.553
Alternatives are leases, CDC-only cache population, or short TTLs as mitigation.

1219
01:05:38.903 --> 01:05:40.034
Question 11.

1220
01:05:40.314 --> 01:05:42.298
How do you handle a hot key in Redis?

1221
01:05:42.578 --> 01:05:43.943
Thirty-second answer:

1222
01:05:44.223 --> 01:05:47.850
Detect it (per-shard metrics, --hotkeys, client-side sampling).

1223
01:05:48.130 --> 01:05:51.437
Absorb repeated reads with an in-process L1 cache.

1224
01:05:51.717 --> 01:05:56.261
Replicate the key under several names on different shards and read a random replica.

1225
01:05:56.541 --> 01:05:59.079
For writes, shard counters or batch updates.

1226
01:05:59.359 --> 01:06:01.855
Pre-identify hot keys for known events.

1227
01:06:03.205 --> 01:06:04.293
Question 12.

1228
01:06:04.573 --> 01:06:09.139
How do you keep in-process (L1) caches consistent across 50 pods?

1229
01:06:09.419 --> 01:06:10.784
Thirty-second answer:

1230
01:06:11.064 --> 01:06:12.899
Short TTLs bound staleness.

1231
01:06:13.179 --> 01:06:24.315
Broadcast invalidation reaches every pod (Kafka with a unique group per pod, or Redis Pub or Sub), and the L2-first ordering makes L1 refills read fresh data.

1232
01:06:24.595 --> 01:06:29.139
Or use Redis client-side caching (tracking), which pushes invalidations.

1233
01:06:30.489 --> 01:06:31.769
Question 13.

1234
01:06:32.049 --> 01:06:36.315
What are cache penetration and cache avalanche, and how do you defend against them?

1235
01:06:36.595 --> 01:06:37.961
Thirty-second answer:

1236
01:06:38.241 --> 01:06:44.811
Penetration: requests for keys that don't exist always miss and hit the origin (bots, bad ids).

1237
01:06:45.091 --> 01:06:52.281
Defend with negative caching (short TTL), Bloom filters of valid ids, validation and rate limits.

1238
01:06:52.561 --> 01:06:55.953
Avalanche: many keys expire or the cache fails at once.

1239
01:06:56.233 --> 01:07:02.675
Defend with TTL jitter, warming, multi-level caches, origin protection, and staggered restarts.

1240
01:07:04.025 --> 01:07:05.220
Question 14.

1241
01:07:05.500 --> 01:07:07.185
Redis goes down during peak.

1242
01:07:07.465 --> 01:07:08.937
What happens in your design?

1243
01:07:09.217 --> 01:07:10.583
Thirty-second answer:

1244
01:07:10.863 --> 01:07:13.551
L1 keeps serving hot keys for its TTL.

1245
01:07:13.831 --> 01:07:27.079
Misses go to the DB through per-pod single-flight, capped by a permit budget, and excess requests get a fast 503 with Retry-After (low-priority traffic first) rather than piling onto the DB.

1246
01:07:27.359 --> 01:07:29.961
Once Redis recovers, it warms gradually.

1247
01:07:30.241 --> 01:07:33.079
Decisions (checkout) never depended on Redis.

1248
01:07:34.429 --> 01:07:35.325
Architect.

1249
01:07:35.605 --> 01:07:36.799
Question 15.

1250
01:07:37.079 --> 01:07:43.287
Design caching for a product catalog serving 190 K req per second at sale start.

1251
01:07:43.567 --> 01:07:44.933
Thirty-second answer:

1252
01:07:45.213 --> 01:07:48.967
CDN for images and anonymous listings (surrogate keys).

1253
01:07:49.247 --> 01:07:54.986
A Caffeine L1 per pod (hot 10 to 50 K keys, 5 s TTL, single-flight).

1254
01:07:55.266 --> 01:08:01.837
Redis L2 with versioned values, XFetch, TTL jitter, and hot-key replicas for sale SKUs.

1255
01:08:02.117 --> 01:08:07.663
Outbox-driven invalidation (L2 tombstones, L1 broadcast, CDN purge).

1256
01:08:07.943 --> 01:08:09.095
Pre-sale warming.

1257
01:08:09.375 --> 01:08:10.975
DB permits and shedding.

1258
01:08:11.255 --> 01:08:13.389
Checkout re-prices from the SoR.

1259
01:08:14.739 --> 01:08:15.933
Question 16.

1260
01:08:16.213 --> 01:08:20.160
How do you define what may be cached and for how long across a platform?

1261
01:08:20.440 --> 01:08:21.805
Thirty-second answer:

1262
01:08:22.085 --> 01:08:37.595
Publish staleness contracts per data class: display data (content, prices, badges) may be cached with bounded staleness and event invalidation, while decision data (checkout price, stock, payment, authorization) always reads the SoR.

1263
01:08:37.875 --> 01:08:47.304
Standardize libraries (a two-level cache component with versioning and metrics), key conventions, and security rules (no personalized public caching).

1264
01:08:48.654 --> 01:08:49.998
Question 17.

1265
01:08:50.278 --> 01:08:52.902
Your cache is now critical to availability.

1266
01:08:53.182 --> 01:08:54.867
How do you reason about that risk?

1267
01:08:55.147 --> 01:08:56.513
Thirty-second answer:

1268
01:08:56.793 --> 01:09:04.622
If the origin can't serve the uncached load, the cache is a hard dependency, and its availability multiplies with the service's.

1269
01:09:04.902 --> 01:09:19.025
Mitigate with a highly available cache (replicas, multi-AZ), L1 as a second layer, origin protection (permits, shedding) so cache loss degrades rather than fails, capacity to rebuild (warming), and game days.

1270
01:09:20.375 --> 01:09:21.548
Question 18.

1271
01:09:21.828 --> 01:09:23.705
When is adding a cache the wrong answer?

1272
01:09:23.985 --> 01:09:25.351
Thirty-second answer:

1273
01:09:25.631 --> 01:09:47.241
When the origin query is slow because of a fixable problem (missing index, N+1), when the data changes faster than it's read (low hit ratio), when correctness requires the latest value (decisions), when the working set is too large or too uniformly accessed to get good hits, or when the invalidation complexity outweighs the gains.

1274
01:09:48.591 --> 01:09:49.829
Question 19.

1275
01:09:50.109 --> 01:09:55.869
How do deploys, scale-outs and cache node replacements affect caching, and how do you plan for them?

1276
01:09:56.149 --> 01:09:57.514
Thirty-second answer:

1277
01:09:57.794 --> 01:10:01.890
Deploys and scale-outs create cold L1s (a miss spike to L2).

1278
01:10:02.170 --> 01:10:07.759
Cache node replacement or reshard creates cold L2 slots (a miss spike to the DB).

1279
01:10:08.039 --> 01:10:10.109
Payload changes need versioned keys.

1280
01:10:10.389 --> 01:10:23.530
Plan with rolling deploys (few pods cold at once), L2 warm for L1, pre-warming for known events, key-prefix versioning, and slot migrations that preserve data (Redis Cluster resharding moves keys).

1281
01:10:24.880 --> 01:10:25.989
Question 20.

1282
01:10:26.269 --> 01:10:31.219
App-managed caching versus CDC-driven cache population: which would you choose?

1283
01:10:31.499 --> 01:10:32.864
Thirty-second answer:

1284
01:10:33.144 --> 01:10:41.272
App-managed (cache-aside + events) is simpler, caches only what's read, and handles misses immediately, but needs race protection.

1285
01:10:41.552 --> 01:10:56.336
CDC-driven (a consumer applies DB changes to the cache in commit order) removes app write paths and races, but must populate everything (or on demand), adds pipeline dependency and lag, and complicates misses.

1286
01:10:57.686 --> 01:10:58.582
Part 9.

1287
01:10:58.862 --> 01:11:00.270
Summary and Practice.

1288
01:11:01.520 --> 01:11:02.608
Key takeaways.

1289
01:11:02.888 --> 01:11:06.365
A cache is a copy with a lifetime and a staleness contract.

1290
01:11:06.645 --> 01:11:08.629
Define the contract per data type.

1291
01:11:08.909 --> 01:11:13.219
The origin is sized for misses: losing the cache multiplies the load.

1292
01:11:13.499 --> 01:11:14.821
Design for that case.

1293
01:11:15.101 --> 01:11:22.760
Cache-aside with delete on write is the default, but add versioned set-if-newer + tombstones to kill the read or write race.

1294
01:11:23.040 --> 01:11:30.592
Stampedes need layered defences: single-flight, XFetch, TTL jitter, warming, and origin permits with shedding.

1295
01:11:31.942 --> 01:11:39.643
Invalidate inside-out (L2, then L1 on all pods, then CDN), driven by outbox or CDC events.

1296
01:11:39.923 --> 01:11:41.737
TTLs are the safety net.

1297
01:11:42.017 --> 01:11:45.558
L1 in-process caches absorb hot keys and save hops.

1298
01:11:45.838 --> 01:11:49.358
Keep them small, with short TTLs and broadcast eviction.

1299
01:11:49.638 --> 01:11:50.577
Hot keys:

1300
01:11:50.857 --> 01:11:54.163
L1 + replicas across shards + event planning.

1301
01:11:54.443 --> 01:12:00.182
Protect against penetration (negative caching, Bloom filters) and avalanche (jitter, warming).

1302
01:12:00.462 --> 01:12:03.619
Display from caches, decide from the system of record.

1303
01:12:04.969 --> 01:12:06.313
Never flush wholesale.

1304
01:12:06.593 --> 01:12:08.343
Version cache keys by schema.

1305
01:12:08.623 --> 01:12:11.652
Never cache personalized responses at shared layers.

1306
